---
import { site } from '@/data/site';
import ArticleLayout from '@/layouts/ArticleLayout.astro';

const published = new Date('2026-10-04');
const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'What if I have no drawing?',
    a: 'Measure the points below, photograph the spring next to a ruler, and send both. Or post an unused spring and we will measure it.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Should I measure a used spring?',
    a: 'You can, but say that it is used. Free length and load are often short after set. An unused spare is better.',
  },
];
---

<ArticleLayout
  route="measure"
  title="How to Measure a Spring"
  description="How to measure wire, OD, free length, coils and ends so we can quote. Metric. Send an unused sample if you can."
  h1="How to measure a spring"
  lede="Five numbers get most quotes moving: wire, outer diameter, free length, coils, and the end or hook type. Metric first."
  published={published}
  context="a measured spring"
  takeaways={[
    'Measure wire diameter, outer diameter, free length and coil count.',
    'Name the ends, hooks or legs. Photograph the spring next to a ruler.',
    'An unused sample beats a used one. We reverse-engineer from a sample if you send it.',
  ]}
  toc={[
    { id: 'tools', text: 'What you need' },
    { id: 'all', text: 'Every spring' },
    { id: 'by-type', text: 'By type' },
    { id: 'send', text: 'What to send' },
    { id: 'questions', text: 'Questions' },
  ]}
  faqs={faqs}
  related={[
    {
      to: 'reverseEngineering',
      label: 'Reverse engineering',
      description: 'Send the sample instead.',
    },
    {
      to: 'compressionCalc',
      label: 'Compression spring calculator',
      description: 'First-pass rate from those numbers.',
    },
    { to: 'rfq', label: 'Request a quote', description: 'Upload the photo or the drawing.' },
  ]}
>
  <h2 id="tools">What you need</h2>
  <p>
    A ruler marked in millimetres, and a way to read wire diameter — a digital caliper is enough for
    a first pass. We do not publish a list of instruments used on this floor.
  </p>

  <h2 id="all">Every spring</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>
      <strong>Wire diameter d.</strong> Measure across the wire, not along it. Rate changes with the
      fourth power of d, so this number matters most.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Outer diameter OD.</strong> Across the coil, outside to outside. If the spring works
      in a hole or over a rod, write that diameter as well.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Free length.</strong> Unloaded. For an extension spring, length inside the hooks. For
      a torsion spring, the free angle between the legs.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Coils.</strong> Count every coil you can see. Say whether that is total coils or
      active coils if you know the difference.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Ends.</strong> Open, closed, closed and ground; hook type; leg shape and wind
      direction.
    </li>
  </ol>

  <h2 id="by-type">By type</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Compression.</strong> Add solid height if you can close it by hand without force, and
    one working length with the load you need there. See the{' '}
    <a href="/springs/compression-springs/">compression spring</a> page.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Extension.</strong> Length inside hooks, hook type and whether the openings face the
    same way. Initial tension if you know it. See{' '}
    <a href="/resources/extension-spring-end-types/">end types</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Torsion / double torsion.</strong> Both leg lengths and shapes, free angle, working
    angle, shaft diameter, wind direction, and the centre-loop width on a double torsion spring.
  </p>

  <h2 id="send">What to send</h2>
  <p>
    A photo next to a ruler, the numbers above, the quantity, and the material if you know it. Or
    post an unused spring. We measure it and quote within 24 hours. Usual minimum {site.terms.moq}.
  </p>
  <p>Wire outside {site.capability.wireRangeLabel} is outside this shop.</p>
</ArticleLayout>
